Ollama v0.35.1 (commit b0c1ca4f): give the free-memory refresh as long as the
GPU library takes to start, so slow-starting GPUs stop running on stale
free-memory values.

WHY
---
Before loading a model, Ollama refreshes each GPU's free memory. When no loaded
runner can answer, it starts a new llama-server just to report device memory,
with a fixed 3-second deadline, and on a miss it keeps the previous values
("unable to refresh free memory, using old values"). On an RX 590 (gfx803,
ROCm) the ROCm library alone takes about 3 s to initialize (llama-server
--list-devices with the HIP backend: 2.8-3.1 s), Ollama's first discovery
pass takes 8.5-9 s per library, and a refresh run takes about 8 s, so every
such refresh misses. The old values then do not change: after a model is
unloaded, Ollama keeps seeing the memory that model held as in use.

Decisions made from those values go wrong. Seen with Qwen3.5 9B and its vision
projector after unloading another model: the card was empty, Ollama believed
1.6 GiB was free, decided VRAM was too limited for the projector
("disabling multimodal projector offload, reason=limited-vram") and encoded
every image on the CPU, at half the speed. A load straight after start-up, when
the values were fresh, put the projector on the GPU.

WHAT
----
The first discovery pass already starts each GPU library once; it now records
the longest that took. A refresh that has to start a new runner gets twice
that, never less than the existing 3 s and at most 30 s. GPUs whose library
starts quickly keep the 3 s deadline. On the RX 590 the refresh gets 18 s and
completes in about 8 s: loading the vision model straight after unloading a
text model now sees 8.0 GiB free and puts the projector on the GPU. (The
scheduler's separate wait for VRAM to recover after an unload still gives up
after its own 5 s and falls back to its estimates, as it is designed to.)
Unit test included; the discover, llm and server package tests pass.

